    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "By incorporating measured offsets along surface ruptures into a boundary element model, this study investigates the effects of the most recent large (M>7) earthquakes along the North Anatolian fault (NAF) in northwestern Turkey on changing the stress conditions on neighboring fault segments. Within the August 1999 Izmit earthquake rupture, right-lateral slip transferred from east-west trending segments near the epicenter onto a pre-existing northeast trending fault to the east before transferring back to an east-west trending segment near the far eastern termination of rupture. Coulomb stress analyses show that the relative orientations of the fault segments favored right-lateral on segments to the east as the slip approached from the western segments. This study also shows that, by accommodating shear slip, nearby small faults can effectively constrain the region of slip on larger faults. On a larger scale, the previous large earthquake in the region that occurred during July of 1967 is shown to have increased the tendency for right- lateral shear on the Izmit fault plane. Modeling results, however, suggest that the Izmit earthquake did not in- crease the tendency for right-lateral shear on the fault plane that ruptured during the November 1999 Düzce earthquake. Slip during the Izmit and Düzce earthquakes has produced a regions of increased Coulomb stress that radiate towards both the east and west from the fault ends. The western region of Coulomb stress increase, in particular, is highly susceptible to a large earthquake as it coincides with a noted seismic gap along the North Anatolian fault.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
